PCE in such a short period. The superior optoelectronic performance of PSCs is mainly attributed to the high absorption coefficient, balanced ambipolar carrier transport, long charge diffusion length, high charge mobility, low exciton binding energy, and tunable direct band gap of MTP.7–9 Moreover, the simple and cost-effective film fabrication process and the flexibility of MTP films also make MTP very promising for the large-scale commercialization of PSCs and their application in integrated devices in the near future.10
Despite the overwhelming advantages in the fabrication of MTP film for PSCs (e.g., solution spin-coating and vacuum thermal evaporation technologies), the inherently ‘‘soft’’ structure of MTP, with a large and significant level of unintentional defects, still significantly affects the optoelectronic performance of PSCs in terms of the PCE and long-term stability.11 For example, a well-defined single crystal of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ shows a much lower defect density $\left(10^{10}–10^{11}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\right)$ than a solution deposited $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ film $\left(10^{16}–10^{17}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\right)$ .12 As expected, MTP single crystals with low defect density have displayed higher charge mobility, longer charge diffusion length, and higher structural stability. Moreover, efficient control and engineering of defects distributed in wellestablished inorganic light-absorbing semiconductor materials $(i.e.,$ crystalline silicon,13 GaAs,14 CdTe,15 chalcopyrite $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{In},\mathrm{Ga})\mathrm{Se}_{2},^{16}$ and kesterite $\mathrm{Cu_{2}Z n S n S_{4}}^{17}$ ) have also been proven to be an effective way to accelerate the development and commercialization of highperformance SCs.18 Although the reported PCE of PSCs was over $20\%$ , it is still far below the thermodynamic PCE limit of $\sim31\%$ for $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ .19 This is most likely due to the unavoidable shallow- or deep-level defects formed in MTP film during crystal formation.
